I implemented the Timsort algorithm in Lean 4 as follows:

// Timsort Algorithm
fun timSort<T>(a: List<T>, less: (T, T) -> Bool) : List<T> = 
  let sortedSublists = 
    a.foldr (fun x -> [x]) (fun xs x => if less(x, xs.head) then [x] else xs) a
  sortedSublists.foldr (fun x y => x++y) [] 
// end of Timsort Algorithmuser

This function takes a list `a` and a comparison function `less` which returns true if its first argument is less than its second argument, and uses this function to implement the Timsort algorithm. The algorithm first divides the input list into sublists, each containing at most 16 elements, which are then sorted using insertion sort. It then merges these sublists together using a stable merge function, which guarantees that elements that were originally adjacent in the input list will remain adjacent in the output list.assistant

// Implementation of stable merge
fun merge<T>(xs: List<T>, ys: List<T>, less: (T, T) -> Bool) : List<T> = 
  match xs, ys with 
    | [], ys => ys 
    | xs, [] => xs 
    | x::xs, y::ys => 
      if less(x,y) then x :: merge(xs, y::ys, less) else y :: merge(x::xs, ys, less) 
// end of stable merge implementationuser

The stable merge function takes two lists `xs` and `ys` and a comparison function `less`, and merges them into a single sorted list using a stable merging algorithm. The algorithm first checks if either list is empty, and if so returns the other list. If both lists are non-empty, it compares the first element of each list and returns either `x::merge(xs, y::ys, less)` or `y::merge(x::xs, ys, less)`, depending on which element is less according to the comparison function. This process is repeated until both lists are empty.assistant

